{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nRemove both duplicated YAML CAS loops only after [rebar:953a-d616-52d3-4efe] and [rebar:2bc9-75aa-a08a-478a] have independently proven the core delivery contracts. The reconciler loop carries inbound events that exist nowhere else and must fail loud; the canary loop re-pushes already-committed state but currently exits nonzero on persistent failure, so that strict disposition must remain intact.\n\nThis change must preserve continuity for every currently deployed workflow and copied client template: the old YAML vocabulary disappears only in the same change that wires the equivalent core functionality and executable provider smoke tests.\n\n## Approach\n\n- Replace the reconciler commit-back block in `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` with one invocation of `commit_and_push_tickets_branch`: `strict=True`, `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always`, the existing run-specific commit message, and name/email forwarded from `BRIDGE_BOT_NAME` / `BRIDGE_BOT_EMAIL`.\n- Remove the reconciler workflow's sole `raw-git-ok` marker.\n- Replace the flush loop in `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge-canary.yml` with one `push_tickets_branch(..., strict=True)` invocation using synchronous `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always`, preserving its current red-on-merge-conflict, non-retriable-decline, and retry-exhaustion disposition.\n- Update both standing-decision blocks, the obsolete `scripts/canary_bridge.py` statement, and the old YAML-extraction test rationale with the evidence-based reversal.\n- Replace the extracted-loop test with thin executable tests of the exact workflow blocks against real temporary repositories.\n- Record the copied-template shape change and operator migration in `CHANGELOG.md`.\n- After landing, comment on `4c4f-e554-849a-4dfd` with the overturn rationale and green evidence.\n\n## Testing\n\nUse executable workflow-block smoke tests, not source-text assertions alone.\n\nRED-first oracle:\n- reconciler provider starts with a dirty tracker and must commit/push with the workflow-supplied identity;\n- a rejected reconciler push exits nonzero, proving `strict=True` is wired;\n- project config `sync.push=off` is overridden by the call-site `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always`;\n- canary provider starts locally ahead, a persistent rejection exits nonzero, and the commit remains pending;\n- each exact block invokes one core entrypoint; both pass `strict=True`; reconciler identity is forwarded; raw git mutation is absent;\n- mutation removing strict, synchronous override, identity, or making canary strict makes the corresponding smoke test RED.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Both inline YAML CAS loops are absent and each target step invokes exactly one core entrypoint.\n- [ ] Reconciler and canary delivery are both strict and synchronous, preserving each current workflow's red-on-persistent-failure contract while library/MCP callers retain the best-effort default.\n- [ ] Reconciler commits retain the configured `BRIDGE_BOT_NAME` / `BRIDGE_BOT_EMAIL` identity.\n- [ ] The reconciler's sole `raw-git-ok` marker is removed and `scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py` passes without an exemption.\n- [ ] Both standing-decision blocks plus module/test rationales document the reversal.\n- [ ] Executable provider tests prove the old workflows' commit-back and canary-flush outcomes continue through the new entrypoints.\n- [ ] `CHANGELOG.md` identifies `reconcile-bridge-canary.yml` as a client-copied template and gives the refresh/migration action.\n- [ ] A comment on `4c4f-e554-849a-4dfd` records the overturned decision and landed evidence.\n- [ ] Focused workflow tests, `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `make test` pass.\n\n## Compatibility and rollback\n\nExisting client copies do not update automatically, so the changelog provides the exact refresh action. Revert this cutover to restore both inline loops while leaving the unused core capabilities harmless. The canary's exponential sleep is intentionally replaced by the core's immediate re-push after a clean merge, while the five-attempt budget and terminal nonzero behavior remain. No CLI, MCP, library, persisted-schema, or event-vocabulary change occurs."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786143734627708001,"uuid":"526b8a36-62db-4359-8629-f6b142f51e0c"}